Gallstones (Cholelithiasis)
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Episodic postprandial RUQ pain, Radiation to right shoulder, Resolution within 6 hours, Mobile echogenic foci on ultrasound

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-023
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of agonizing upper right abdominal pain causing the patient to pace the floor, occurring shortly after a heavy evening meal.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, adult child.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute, severe right upper quadrant pain accompanied by vomiting and extreme restlessness. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to rule out acute cholecystitis, ascending cholangitis, or severe acute pancreatitis, and to provide immediate parenteral analgesia to manage the extreme distress prior to the next available in-hours consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The pain hit me about an hour after eating a burger, it is gripping my ribs and shooting straight up into my right shoulder."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a sudden onset of biliary colic presenting as severe, episodic right upper quadrant and epigastric pain commencing 2 hours prior to the assessment. The pain radiates intensely to the right subscapular region. Onset occurred approximately 45 minutes after consuming a high-fat meal. Associated with severe nausea and one episode of non-bilious vomiting. Patient reports extreme restlessness during the attack. Complete absence of fever or preceding systemic inflammatory signs. Reports similar, milder episodes in the past that completely spontaneously resolved within 3 to 4 hours.
Functional Status: Pacing the room continuously, unable to sit or lie still due to the severity of the pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme pain preventing any rest or oral intake.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 (Pain mediated) | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient is in acute, severe distress, diaphoretic, and extremely restless, frequently shifting positions.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals focal right upper quadrant tenderness on deep palpation. Crucially, there is an absolute absence of localized guarding, rigidity, or rebound peritonism. Murphy's sign is negative (no inspiratory arrest on deep palpation). No scleral icterus or clinical jaundice observed. The presentation is classic for episodic postprandial RUQ pain, radiation to the right shoulder, and expected resolution within 6 hours; mobile echogenic foci on ultrasound are presumed pending outpatient imaging.
Environmental Observations: Discarded fast-food wrappers on the kitchen counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications. Took oral paracetamol 1 hour ago with zero effect on the pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Biliary Colic (Cholelithiasis) - Status: Exacerbated (Acute attack)
Differentials:

Acute Cholecystitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of a positive Murphy's sign, lack of fever, and absence of localized rigid peritonism, indicating the gallbladder is obstructed but not yet acutely infected.

Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain localized strictly to the right upper quadrant radiating to the right scapula, rather than central crushing chest pain, and a clear relationship to a recent high-fat meal.

Peptic Ulcer Perforation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The completely soft, non-rigid abdomen between waves of pain, rather than the classic board-like abdomen of a perforated viscus.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a textbook manifestation of acute biliary colic, driven by transient gallstone impaction in the cystic duct following a fatty meal. The extreme severity of the pain and associated tachycardia necessitated urgent out-of-hours evaluation to definitively rule out life-threatening complications such as acute cholecystitis or biliary sepsis (cholangitis). Physical examination confirmed the absence of peritonitis or fever. Consequently, the patient required immediate administration of potent parenteral analgesia to terminate the severe pain cycle, allowing them to remain safely at home and avoiding an unnecessary emergency department admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ketorolac 30mg and IM Ondansetron 4mg, resulting in significant reduction of pain within 30 minutes. Arranged a non-urgent outpatient transabdominal ultrasound.
Positive Behavioral Change: Strictly eliminate all high-fat and fried foods from your diet starting immediately. Consuming fat triggers a hormonal signal that forces your gallbladder to squeeze violently; by removing the fat, you prevent the gallbladder from contracting and physically pushing another stone into the duct, thereby preventing another agonizing attack.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to coordinate ultrasound results and surgical referral for elective cholecystectomy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the severe pain returns and lasts continuously for more than 6 hours, or if you develop a fever, severe shivering, or notice your eyes turning yellow, present immediately to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The pain has settled, but the stones remain in the gallbladder. Definitive treatment usually requires surgical removal of the gallbladder.
Clinical Course: Attacks are likely to recur if dietary triggers are not strictly avoided. The current attack should completely resolve within the next few hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The injection has temporarily relieved the spasm. If the agonizing pain returns and does not stop, or if you begin vomiting uncontrollably, you must go to the hospital to ensure the stone has not become permanently stuck.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A solid gallstone temporarily wedged into the narrow exit tube of your gallbladder when it squeezed to digest your recent meal. The intense pain was your gallbladder severely cramping against this blockage. The injection provided powerful anti-inflammatory pain relief that relaxed the duct, allowing the stone to fall back into the gallbladder.
Actionable Steps: Consume only clear liquids for the rest of tonight, and transition to a strictly low-fat diet tomorrow. Do not eat any fast food, full-fat dairy, or heavy meats, as these will directly cause the gallbladder to violently contract and trigger another immediate attack.

